2. THE MAXIMAL-REPRESENTATIVE LAW
   For a random function, a class has R~Poisson(1) roots; P(u is the max of its
   class) = sum_j e^-1/j! * x^j = e^{x-1}.  Normalising: f(x)=e^{x-1}/(1-e^{-1}).
   predicted p(bit31=1) = (1-e^{-1/2})/(1-e^{-1}) = 0.62246
     W1 measured 0.62292   E[W]/2^32 pred 0.58198 meas 0.58196
     W2 measured 0.62345   E[W]/2^32 pred 0.58198 meas 0.58225
     W3 measured 0.62368   E[W]/2^32 pred 0.58198 meas 0.58164
   TOTAL entropy deficit of this law = 0.05864 bits (out of 32)
   fraction of classes non-empty = 1-e^{-1} = 0.63212  (matches the quoted 0.634 hit rate)
   solutions reached per swept a0 = (1-e^{-1})^3 = 0.25258 = 2^-1.985  -> 1.985 bits discarded

